NAME
       pivim — Vim modal editor extension for Pi

DESCRIPTION
       pivim  is  a  Neovim-inspired  modal  editor  that  replaces  Pi's
       default editor with a Vi-like  interface.  It  provides  modes,  an
       operator-motion  grammar,  macros,  registers,  visual  selection, a
       command line, and a status bar widget.

MODES
       The editor operates in four modes:

       INSERT (default on session start)
            Characters typed are inserted into the buffer. Escape  returns
            to NORMAL mode.

       NORMAL
            Commands  navigate  and  manipulate  text.  This is the anchor
            mode.

       VISUAL
            Text is selected  by  extending  cursor  motion.  Press  v  to
            enter character-visual, V for line-visual. Escape deselects.

       COMMAND
            Entered  by  pressing  : in NORMAL mode. Commands are executed
            on Enter; Escape aborts.

NORMAL MODE COMMANDS
   Cursor motions
       h, j, k, l    Left, down, up, right (with count prefix)

   Word motions
       w, W          Forward to next word start.  W  uses  whitespace  as
                     word separator.

       b, B          Backward to previous word start.

       e, E          Forward to end of word (inclusive). E uses whitespace
                     as word separator.

       Neovim  semantics:  empty  lines  act  as  word  boundaries;  cw/CW
       becomes ce/CE when the cursor is on a word character.

   Line motions
       0             Jump to column 0.

       ^             First non-blank character on the line.

       $             End of line (inclusive, one step left of last char).

       _             First non-blank, count lines down (like Neovim).

   Page and screen
       Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B
                     Page down, page up.

       Ctrl-D, Ctrl-U
                     Half-page down, half-page up (approximate).

       H, M, L       Jump to top, middle, bottom of visible screen. H  and
                     L accept a count to offset from the edge.

       zt, zz, zb   Scroll so cursor is at top, center, or bottom of
                     visible area (also z., z-).

   Paragraph motions
       {, }          Jump to previous/next paragraph  (blank  line).  Neo‐
                     vim findpar semantics: passes through non-blank lines
                     then stops at the blank line. Cursor ends at column 0.

   Bracket matching
       %             Jump to matching bracket (( ) [ ] { } < >).  If  cur‐
                     sor  is not on a bracket, scans forward on the cur‐
                     rent line to find one, then matches it.

   Jump to line
       G             Jump to last line (with count: jump to that line).

       gg            Jump to first line (with count: jump to that line).

   Screen scroll
       z<CR>, zt     Scroll cursor to top of screen.

       z., zz        Scroll cursor to center of screen.

       zb, z-        Scroll cursor to bottom of screen.

   Character search motions
       f{char}       Forward to next occurrence of char (inclusive).

       F{char}       Backward to previous occurrence (inclusive).

       t{char}       Forward to just before next occurrence.

       T{char}       Backward to just after previous occurrence.

   Operators
       These operators prefix a motion or text object to act on a range:

       d             Delete the range.

       c             Delete the range and enter INSERT mode.

       y             Yank (copy) the range into the unnamed register.

       >             Indent the range (insert 4 spaces).

       <             Outdent the range (remove up to 4 spaces).

       Double-tap  an  operator (dd, cc, yy, >>, <<) to act on the entire
       current line (count repeats the line).

   Linewise commands (count-aware)
       dd            Delete count lines.

       cc            Delete count lines and enter INSERT mode.

       yy or Y       Yank count lines (Neovim: Y = yy).

       >>            Indent count lines.

       <<            Outdent count lines.

   Self-contained commands
       x             Delete the character under the cursor  (count  re‐
                     peats).

       X             Delete the character before the cursor  (count  re‐
                     peats).

       D             Delete from cursor to end of line.

       C             Delete from cursor to end of line and  enter  INSERT
                     mode (like c$).

       s             Substitute count characters (cl) and enter INSERT.

       S             Substitute count lines (cc) and enter INSERT.

       r{char}       Replace  count  characters  with  the given char (no
                     INSERT mode).

       J             Join line below with current line (count:  join  N-1
                     lines).  Inserts  one  space  if  the  current  line
                     doesn't end in whitespace.

   Insertion commands (enter INSERT mode)
       i             Insert before cursor.

       I             Insert at first non-blank of the line.

       a             Insert after cursor.

       A             Insert at end of the line.

       o             Open a new line below and insert.

       O             Open a new line above and insert.

   Undo / Redo
       u             Undo the last change (count repeats).

       Ctrl-R        Redo the last undone change (count repeats).

   Put (paste)
       p             Put yanked/deleted text after cursor.

       P             Put before cursor.

       Linewise registers are inserted on a new line; charwise  registers
       are inserted inline.

   Text objects (after an operator)
       iw            Inner word (excludes surrounding whitespace).

       aw            A word (includes surrounding whitespace).

       i(, i), ib    Inner parentheses block.

       a(, a), ab    A parentheses block (includes brackets).

       i{, i}, iB    Inner curly-brace block.

       a{, a}, aB    A curly-brace block.

       i[, i]        Inner square-bracket block.

       a[, a]        A square-bracket block.

       i<, i>        Inner angle-bracket block.

       a<, a>        An angle-bracket block.

       i", a"        Inner / a double-quoted string.

       i', a'        Inner / a single-quoted string.

       i`, a`        Inner / a backtick-quoted string.

       Range includes the type  modifiers  for  block  matching:  (,  ),
       {,  },  [,  ], <, >, b (parentheses), B (braces) are all accepted
       as the second key.

   Registers
       "{reg}        Prefix an operator (d, c, y, p, P) with  "a  …  "z
                     to  store/retrieve  from  a  named  register.  (Cur‐
                     rently registers are stored but  yank/put  only  uses
                     the unnamed register.)

   Macros
       q{reg}        Start recording keystrokes into register {reg}.  All
                     subsequent  NORMAL-mode  keys  are  captured.   Press
                     q again to stop recording.

       @{reg}        Play back the macro stored in register {reg}.

       Recording  state  is  displayed  in  the  status  bar  as  a  red
       ● Rec @{reg} indicator.

   Repeat last change
       .             Repeat the last editing  operation  (supports  line‐
                     wise, char-range, visual, and simple operations).

   Visual mode
       v             Start character-wise visual selection.  Motions  ex‐
                     tend  the  selection.  Press  v again to return to
                     NORMAL mode.

       V             Start line-wise visual selection.  Press  V  again
                     to return to NORMAL mode.

       In visual mode, the following operators act on the selection:
            d or x    Delete selection and return to NORMAL.
            c         Delete selection and enter INSERT.
            y         Yank selection and return to NORMAL.
            >         Indent selected lines.
            <         Outdent selected lines.

       Text objects (i/a) can also extend a visual selection.

       gv            (Not yet implemented — re-selects last visual area.)

COMMAND MODE
       :w            Save the current buffer state to ~/.pi_vim_save.json
                     for later resume.

       :wq, :x       Save and exit the editor process.

       :q            Exit the editor (without saving).

       :q!           Force exit the editor.

       :man          Open the pivim man page in less.

       :!{command}   Execute a bash command (sends the command text to Pi
                     for processing).

       :s/old/new[/flags]
                     Substitute  text  on  the current line. Flags: g for
                     global (all occurrences), c for confirm (skipped for
                     now).

       :%s/old/new[/flags]
                     Substitute across all lines.

STATUS PERSISTENCE
       The :w and :wq commands write the current editor text  and  cursor
       position  to  ~/.pi_vim_save.json.  Pi  can  resume  this state on
       restart by reading the file (extension-level feature).

